    "pageType": "text",
    "text": "CHAPTER 1\nChapter 1: There Are No Before\nPictures\nEvery fitness program shows you a before picture.\nI can't. Because none exist.\nI grew up skinny fat. I can't remember a single time in my childhood when I was lean. I had\nboobs. In clothes, I could hide them. That was the one advantage I had, and I used it every single\nday. I wore sweaters even in the summer.\nThe pool and the beach? I avoided them completely, even though I wanted to go. I was not going\nto let people see my boobs, and I couldn't just keep a shirt on in the water either. A regular fat\nperson keeps his shirt on and everyone understands why. A skinny fat person keeps his shirt on\nand nobody understands. In clothes I looked normal, so what exactly was I hiding? I had no\nanswer people would accept. So I stayed home.\nThat's why the \"before\" photo of me isn't a picture. It's the absence of one. I made sure no\ncamera ever caught me without a shirt. If you've ever dodged a camera, worn layers in the heat,\nor made an excuse to skip the pool, you already know exactly what I looked like. You look like\nthat in your own head right now.\nThat's why I wrote this.\nHow Bad Was It, Actually?\nLet me give you the real number, because I've done the math.\nToday I'm 28 years old, 135 pounds, at 14% body fat. That means I carry about 116 pounds of\nlean mass and about 19 pounds of fat.\nAt 22, I weighed 185 pounds. Back then I had at least 10 pounds less muscle than I have now,\nso my lean mass was around 106 pounds at most. Do the subtraction: that leaves roughly 79\npounds of pure fat on my body.\nThat's about 43% body fat.\nFor a man, healthy is 10 to 20%. Obesity starts at 25%. I was carrying nearly double the obesity\nthreshold, at 22 years old, while looking \"normal\" in a sweater. Nearly half of my body weight was\nfat. That's the trap of skinny fat: the mirror in clothes says you're fine while your body\ncomposition says you're in serious metabolic danger.",
    "title": "Chapter 1: There Are No Before",
