    "pageType": "text",
    "text": "The Wake Up Call\nAt 22, I had an accident. I won't dress it up. It did what accidents do. It made me realize nobody\nwas coming to fix my life. If I wanted the body I'd been wishing for since I was a kid, I had to take\ncontrol and work for it.\nSo I did. Sort of.\nFour Years of Doing It Wrong\nYears 1 and 2: I threw myself into calisthenics. I ate high protein, or at least I think I did, because\nI never tracked anything. I lost 20 pounds from 185. Then I'd yo yo it back.\nYears 3 and 4: I stopped going to the gym and just ate carefully. Kind of. I lost another 10\npounds over those two years. Still yo yoing. Still not tracking. Still not walking.\nHere's what the yo yoing really was. It came directly from being skinny fat. Some days I'd look at\nmy arms, feel too weak and skinny, and increase my calories to build muscle. Then I'd catch my\nstomach in the mirror, feel too fat, and cut my calories back down. Up and down, over and over,\nbecause I never knew which problem to fix. Too skinny to bulk. Too fat to feel good shirtless.\nChasing both goals at once and reaching neither.\nFour years. Thirty pounds. I still looked soft, and people commented on it.\nIf you're there right now, I need you to hear this: it wasn't a willpower problem. I worked hard for\nfour years. I was just working blind.\nThe Sentence That Changed Everything\nThen I found Brandon Carter, and one line hit me like a brick:\n\"If you ain't tracking, you slacking.\"\nHe preached two things: you can't out train a bad diet, and get lean first, then build muscle. I\nfinally listened.\nHere's the math that made it real for me. I'm a recovering chocolate chip cookie addict. One\ncookie takes about 10,000 steps to burn off. At my peak, I'd eat a whole 12 pack. That's 120,000\nsteps. There aren't enough hours in a day to walk that off. You cannot out walk a cookie habit.\nYou can't out train any of it. The only lever that works is knowing your numbers.\n90 Days vs. 2 Years",
    "title": "How to Lose Fat as Fast as Possible",
