    "pageType": "text",
    "text": "Now the floor. No matter what the formula says, your daily food intake never goes below 1,500\ncalories for men or 1,200 for women. Below that, you lose muscle, energy, and your ability to\nstick with this. Check it:\nMaintenance minus max deficit = ________ calories eaten per day\nIf that number lands under your floor, shrink your deficit until eating lands at the floor. Mike: 2,850\nminus 1,643 = 1,207. That's under the 1,500 floor, so Mike caps his deficit at 1,350 and eats\n1,500 a day at full speed.\nStep 5: Pick Your Speed\nYour deficit lives between two ends:\nThe patient end: 500 per day. About a pound a week. You'll barely feel it. More food, more\nenergy, easier social life, maximum muscle kept. If your timeline is flexible, this is the smart play.\nThe full send: your personal max. This is what I did. My numbers allow a 1,500 daily deficit,\nand I used every bit of it because I was done being the guy in the sweater. It works. I'll also tell\nyou straight: it demands real discipline, and tracking is what makes it survivable.\nOr anything in between. This is your call, and it's a math decision, not a willpower decision.\nPick the timeline you want, then check it against your max.\nMy deficit: ________ calories per day My daily intake: maintenance minus deficit = ________\ncalories per day\nStep 6: Your Exact Timeline\nOne pound of fat holds about 3,500 calories. So:\nDays to goal = (pounds to lose × 3,500) ÷ daily deficit\nMike wants to lose 29 lbs at a 1,350 deficit: (29 × 3,500) ÷ 1,350 = 75 days. Two and a half\nmonths to a body Mike has never had in his life.\nRun yours:\n(________ lbs × 3,500) ÷ ________ deficit = ________ days\nCircle that number. Put a date on your calendar. That's the day you meet the version of you that\nthis program builds.\nThe Fine Print That Makes This Real",
