    "pageType": "text",
    "text": "controls YOU breaks you. Ego adds weight to the bar, form adds muscle to your body.\nPush Day\n1. Bench press (barbell, dumbbell, or chest press machine): 3 sets of 6 to 10\n2. Seated shoulder press (dumbbell or machine): 3 sets of 8 to 12\n3. Incline dumbbell press (or incline machine): 3 sets of 8 to 12\n4. Lateral raises (dumbbell or cable): 3 sets of 12 to 15\n5. Triceps pushdown (cable): 3 sets of 10 to 15\nPull Day\n1. Lat pulldown (or assisted pull ups): 3 sets of 6 to 10\n2. Seated cable row (or machine row): 3 sets of 8 to 12\n3. One arm dumbbell row: 3 sets of 8 to 12 per side\n4. Rear delt fly (machine or dumbbell): 3 sets of 12 to 15\n5. Biceps curls (dumbbell or cable): 3 sets of 10 to 15\nLegs Day\n1. Squat (barbell, goblet, or leg press): 3 sets of 6 to 10\n2. Romanian deadlift (dumbbell or barbell): 3 sets of 8 to 12\n3. Leg extension: 3 sets of 10 to 15\n4. Lying or seated leg curl: 3 sets of 10 to 15\n5. Standing calf raises: 3 sets of 12 to 15\n6. Plank: 3 rounds, hold as long as you can with a straight line\nToo Nervous to Walk In? I Was Too.\nReal talk: I struggled with feeling too nervous to go to the gym. Everyone in there seemed to\nknow what they were doing, and I was sure they'd all be watching the skinny fat guy fumble with\nthe machines. If that's you, two things.\nFirst, nobody is watching you. Everyone in that gym is staring at themselves in the mirror or at\ntheir phone between sets. The judgment you're afraid of exists only in your head. I know because\nI was certain of it too, and it was never real.\nSecond, if the nerves still win, start with bodyweight at home. Push ups, squats, lunges, planks,\nrows under a sturdy table. That's how I started my first two years. It absolutely builds muscle, and\nit builds the confidence to walk into the gym later. The gym is the optimal endgame for one",
