    "text": "reason: progress tracking. A push up is hard to measure. A bar with numbers on it turns every\nworkout into a score you can beat, and beating your score is the engine of this whole program.\nStart where your nerves allow, aim yourself at the gym.\nThe Only Three Rules\nRule 1: Progress or it doesn't count. Each exercise has a rep range. Start with a weight where\nyou can hit the bottom of the range with good form. Every workout, try to add a rep. When you hit\nthe top of the range on all three sets, add the smallest weight jump available and start again at\nthe bottom. That's progressive overload, and it's the entire science of building strength in one\nparagraph.\nRule 2: Rest enough to actually lift. Two to three minutes after the big lifts (bench, squat, rows,\npulldowns), 60 to 90 seconds after the small ones. Rushing rest doesn't burn meaningful\ncalories, it just makes your next set weaker. The treadmill handles cardio. The gym handles\nmuscle. Don't mix the jobs.\nRule 3: Log every workout. Same principle as food. Weight and reps for every set, in your\nphone or on the printable sheet at the end of this chapter. Next session, look at last session's\nnumbers and beat one of them. If you ain't tracking, you slacking applies to the bar too.\nWhat to Expect While Cutting\nSet your expectations now so nothing surprises you: in a calorie deficit, you're not going to set\npowerlifting records. Early on, especially if you're new to lifting, your strength will climb anyway.\nDeeper into an aggressive cut, some lifts will stall. On a cut, maintaining your strength IS\nwinning. The scale dropping while your lifts hold steady means everything you're losing is fat.\nThat's the exact outcome this whole book is built for.\nMiss a workout? Just hit the next one. Three days has enough slack in it that life can happen.\nZero days for two weeks is the only real failure.\nYour Week at Full Speed\nHere's the whole physical program on one line: lift three days, hit 10,000 steps every day,\nstay under your calorie number. A person who does those three things for 90 days does not\nkeep their old body. It's not possible.\nOne more excuse to kill before it forms: \"I don't have time.\" Three hours a week is under 2% of\nyour waking life, and Chapter 4 already showed you the steps happen during time you currently\ngive to your phone. And if you're somehow the busiest person in the world? Drop and do push\nups. At home, in your bedroom, three times a week. No commute, no membership, no excuse\nleft.",
